# Missile ids above 255 (O737; server/README.md "Missile ids above 255 (O737)"). A distance effect (missile, 0x85) was one byte all the way:
# ShootType_t, CombatParams::distanceEffect, Game::addDistanceEffect, Player / ProtocolGame::sendDistanceShoot, the overrides of 0045. Pair 81
# uses all 253 missiles, so no new projectile could be imported. Now the id is 16 bits in the engine, and the wire follows the client:
#   * Login: after the capability byte of 0051 ("C" + u8) our client adds, inside the same RSA block and only when it fits, the string "W"
#     and a second capability byte; bit 0 asks for 16-bit missile ids (client base patch 0208). The server reads those 4 bytes only inside
#     the block, and only u16 length 1 + 'W' + bit 0 counts as asking, so zero or random padding never asks (src/chaoswidemissile.h,
#     tested by server/tools/chaoswidemissile_test.cpp).
#   * sendFeatures: a client that asked gets the engine's own feature 77 (GameDistanceEffectU16) in the 0x43 list; the client engine
#     already reads a u16 missile under it. Every 0x85 to that client after the list carries a u16 id.
#   * Any other client (an older build of ours, another client, the Unity client without the flag) gets one byte: an id up to 255 as it
#     is, an id above 255 as its fallback set from Lua (Game.setMissileFallback(id, fallback), data/lib/chaos_wide_missiles.lua) or
#     nothing at all. Never a broken packet.
#   * The live cast hello frame (0064) gets flag 4 (HELLO_WIDE_MISSILES) so cast_relay.py reads the spectator's 0x85 right.
